#{[17592186321019 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1)=4.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}-1})={\\color{blue}4}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{x} \\\\ {\\color{blue}4} & =A{e}^{{\\color{red}-1}} \\\\ 4 & =A{e}^{-1} \\\\ 4e & =A \\\\ A & =4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321021 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}2})={\\color{blue}\\frac{3}{4}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}\\frac{3}{4}} & =\\frac{A}{{{\\color{red}2}}^{4}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{A}{16} \\\\ A & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321022 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A-x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=3.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}0})={\\color{blue}3}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{A-x} \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & =\\frac{1}{A-{\\color{red}0}} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{A} \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321023 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{x}^{3}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=4.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}2})={\\color{blue}4}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}4} & =A({\\color{red}2})^{3} \\\\ 4 & =A(8) \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373785 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-2)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}-2})={\\color{blue}-1}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{x} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-1} & =\\frac{A}{({\\color{red}-2})} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$"] [17592186381209 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)-{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡x)-{y}^{2}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =-{y}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡x-2x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =-(1)^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-\\pi  \\\\  & =-1-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186381216 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({3}^{x}\\cdot {3}^{y}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)-{3}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({3}^{y}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x}\\cdot {3}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186381218 :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}"] [17592186381220 :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] [17592186381221 :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] [17592186381244 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\cdot (x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\cdot (x\\cdot 0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186381247 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}"] [17592186381248 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}"] [17592186381249 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}+1"] [17592186381250 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x+y)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}"] [17592186381286 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})) & =(2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi /4,1)} & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {1}^{2})+{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186385185 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty ,$ we require $a>0.$"] [17592186391460 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that the given curve is $\\text{bounded}$ (and closed), representing $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nBut we have that:\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a>0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a<0.$\n\nThus, we must have $a=0.$"] [17592186398445 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}2})={\\color{blue}-1}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-1} & =\\frac{A}{{{\\color{red}2}}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{A}{4} \\\\ A & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186398452 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}1})={\\color{blue}1}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}(A+x) \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & =\\operatorname{ln}(A+{\\color{red}1}) \\\\ {e}^{1} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(A+1)} \\\\ e & =A+1 \\\\ A & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e-1+x).\n$$"] [17592186398459 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A+x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}0})={\\color{blue}\\frac{1}{2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{A+x} \\\\ {\\color{blue}\\frac{1}{2}} & =\\frac{1}{A+{\\color{red}0}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{A} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2+x}.\n$$"] [17592186399657 :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy.\n$$"] [17592186399657 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{{y}^{-1}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$\n\nUse intervals with $x\\ne0$ and $C-x^3\\ne0$. The original equation also admits $y=0$, outside the requested nonzero family."] [17592186436604 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(0,\\sqrt{\\pi }).$"] [17592186436604 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (3x\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (3x\\cdot 0-2y) \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,\\sqrt{\\pi }):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(0,\\sqrt{\\pi })} & =4(\\sqrt{\\pi })\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(0)-{(\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(0)-{(\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
